## Grades 2-3 / 4-5 Problem 3: is each printed ring the result of one move?
  Completeness of the search: predecessors are closed under adding a constant, so translate one to
  minimum 0; walking both ways round the ring from a lowest to a highest station gives
  2*(max - min) <= sum of the four gaps, so heights 0..floor(sum/2) cover every normalised predecessor.
  2-3 (2,0,2,0): total 4 (even); normalised predecessors ['(0,2,2,0)', '(2,0,0,2)']
  2-3 (1,1,1,0): total 3 (odd); normalised predecessors none
  2-3 (1,1,1,1): total 4 (even); normalised predecessors ['(0,1,0,1)', '(0,1,2,1)', '(1,0,1,0)', '(1,0,1,2)', '(1,2,1,0)', '(2,1,0,1)']
  2-3 (0,0,0,2): total 2 (even); normalised predecessors none
  4-5 (2,0,2,0): total 4 (even); normalised predecessors ['(0,2,2,0)', '(2,0,0,2)']
  4-5 (1,1,1,0): total 3 (odd); normalised predecessors none
  4-5 (1,1,1,1): total 4 (even); normalised predecessors ['(0,1,0,1)', '(0,1,2,1)', '(1,0,1,0)', '(1,0,1,2)', '(1,2,1,0)', '(2,1,0,1)']
  4-5 (0,0,0,2): total 2 (even); normalised predecessors none
[OK  ] guide witness (0,2,2,0) -> (2,0,2,0)
[OK  ] guide witness (0,1,0,1) -> (1,1,1,1)
[OK  ] (1,1,1,0) has no predecessor (guide: impossible)
[OK  ] (0,0,0,2) has no predecessor (guide: impossible)
[OK  ] every output has an even total (0..7 exhaustive)
[OK  ] (0,0,0,2) has an even total yet no predecessor: "necessary, not sufficient" is right

